<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domini di Terraferma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1339">1339</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Venetian Terraferma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Domini di Terraferma were the mainland territories of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, conquered mainly between 1339 and 1428 and including Treviso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and Bergamo, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>. They kept their own statutes and councils under Venetian governors. Lost almost entirely after Agnadello in 1509, they were mostly recovered by 1517 and held until 1797.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domini-di-terraferma.jpg" alt="Domini di Terraferma" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: it.wikipedia.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Repubblica_Venezia_espansione_in_Terraferma.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Domini di Terraferma, also called the Stato da Tera, were one of the three parts of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s possessions, beside the original Dogado around the lagoon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Màr</a>, the maritime territories. At their widest they covered the present-day region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, the western and central parts of Friuli-Venezia Giulia and the provinces of Bergamo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>.</p><p>In the west they reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda-river">Adda River</a>, where they bordered the Imperial Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In the south the lower Po, the Polesine, formed the border with the Papal States, and in the north the Carnic and Julian Alps separated them from the Inner Austrian duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> the easternmost part along the Isonzo River belonged to the Imperial counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> took the Mestre mainland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> lords of Verona in 1337 during the Scaliger War, and Treviso and Bassano del Grappa in 1339. Systematic expansion began with Doge Michele Steno from 1400, who campaigned in the hinterland to secure trade and food for the city. His successors Tommaso Mocenigo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari">Francesco Foscari</a> extended the territory at the expense of the Scaligeri, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carraresi">Carraresi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, whose lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-novello-da-carrara">Francesco Novello da Carrara</a> was executed in 1406, and the Visconti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><p>In 1420 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> annexed the Friulian lands of the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> from the Adriatic coast up to Pontebba in the Julian Alps. Emperor Sigismund acknowledged the conquest in 1433 and four years later ceded the territory to Venice as an Imperial fief. In 1523 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> finally gave up all his titles as feudal lord.</p><p>With the fall of the Republic the Domini passed briefly under French rule until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ceded them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in 1797. They were joined to the Napoleonic Kingdom of Italy in 1805, to the Austrian Kingdom of Lombardy–Venetia in 1815, and to the Kingdom of Italy in 1866 as a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-italian-war-of-independence">Third Italian War of Independence</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domini di Terraferma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The mainland <b>Terraferma</b>, conquered between 1339 and 1428, including Treviso, Padua, Vicenza, Verona, Brescia and Bergamo, kept its own statutes and local councils under Venetian rectors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
